[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a **step‑by‑step recipe** that turns “green disappears in the forest / brown disappears on the field” into **icons that stay readable on any satellite or aerial image**.  
All the tricks come from the *MapLibre‑Cartography* skill – halo/outline, SDF sprites, contact‑shadows, colour choice, and layer ordering.

---

## 1. Choose the right visual language for “any background”

| Problem | Why it happens | What works on imagery |
|---------|----------------|-----------------------|
| **Camouflage** – the icon’s hue is present in the photo (green = trees, brown = soil) | The photo supplies the dominant **chroma**; a flat‑filled icon has no contrast on that axis. | Use **high‑chroma, neutral‑hue** fills (amber, terracotta, teal) that are *rare* in natural scenes, and add a **halo/outline** that gives a second contrast axis (light‑vs‑dark). |
| **Busy background** – texture, roofs, water, shadows | The eye can’t separate the icon edge from the photo. | Add a **thin halo** (or a “contact shadow”) that creates a clear edge regardless of what’s behind it. |
| **Flat PNG icons** | No halo property – the edge is baked in. | Either **bake the halo/outline into the PNG** (extra padding, a thin stroke) **or** switch to an **SDF sprite** (recommended). |

---

## 2. Build an SDF sprite (the easiest way to get a halo)

1. **Create a single‑color SVG** (black fill, no stroke).  
   ```svg
   <svg viewBox="0 0 64 64" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
     <path d="M32 2 L58 58 L6 58 Z"/>   <!-- example triangle -->
   </svg>
   ```
2. **Export it as a 256 × 256 PNG** (or keep it as SVG – MapLibre can rasterise it at runtime).  
3. **Add it to the map as an SDF image**:

   ```js
   // fetch the SVG, turn it into an Image, then add it
   fetch('icons/marker.svg')
     .then(r => r.text())
     .then(svg => {
       const img = new Image();
       const svgBlob = new Blob([svg], {type: 'image/svg+xml'});
       const url = URL.createObjectURL(svgBlob);
       img.onload = () => {
         map.addImage('marker-sdf', img, { sdf: true, pixelRatio: 2 });
         URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
       };
       img.src = url;
     });
   ```

   *Why `sdf: true`?* → It tells MapLibre that the image is a signed‑distance‑field, which enables `icon-halo-*` and `icon-color` paint properties.

4. **Add a thin halo** (the “figure‑ground” edge):

   ```json
   {
     "id": "my-points",
     "type": "symbol",
     "source": "my-points-src",
     "layout": {
       "icon-image": "marker-sdf",
       "icon-size": 1.0,
       "icon-allow-overlap": true,
       "icon-ignore-placement": false
     },
     "paint": {
       "icon-color": "#e68a00",               // amber – high chroma, neutral hue
       "icon-halo-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.75)", // dark semi‑transparent halo
       "icon-halo-width": 2,                  // 2 px works well at 1× size
       "icon-halo-blur": 0.5
     }
   }
   ```

   *Result*: The amber fill gives a colour that rarely appears in the photo, while the dark halo creates a crisp edge that separates the icon from any bright or dark background.

---

## 3. If you must keep a raster PNG (e.g., a logo)

1. **Add a 1‑pixel stroke** around the shape in a colour that contrasts with the fill (e.g., white stroke on a dark icon, black stroke on a light icon).  
2. **Add a faint outer glow** in the same colour as the halo you would use for an SDF icon (e.g., a 2‑pixel black glow with 75 % opacity).  
3. **Pad the image** (extra transparent pixels) so the halo isn’t clipped.  

> **Tip:** You can generate the same effect automatically with ImageMagick:  
> `convert source.svg -border 4 -background none -fill "#e68a00" -stroke "#00000080" -strokewidth 2 miff:- | convert - -background none -gravity center -extent 256x256 marker.png`

---

## 4. Add a **contact‑shadow** (optional but looks great on imagery)

A contact‑shadow is a soft, flattened ellipse under the icon that “anchors” it to the ground. It works better than a drop‑shadow because it follows the terrain’s illumination direction.

```json
{
  "id": "my-points-shadow",
  "type": "symbol",
  "source": "my-points-src",
  "layout": {
    "icon-image": "circle-sdf",          // a simple SDF circle (no fill)
    "icon-size": 0.6,
    "icon-allow-overlap": true
  },
  "paint": {
    "icon-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.4)",     // dark, semi‑transparent
    "icon-halo-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0)",  // no halo for the shadow
    "icon-translate": [0, 6],            // shift down a few pixels (pixel‑units)
    "icon-translate-anchor": "map"
  }
}
```

Place the shadow **below** the real icon (add it first in the `layers` array, or insert it before the icon layer with `addLayer`).

---

## 5. Layer ordering – keep your icons **under** basemap labels but **over** the imagery

```js
// 1️⃣ add the raster imagery (already in the style)
// 2️⃣ add your point‑shadow layer (if you use one)
map.addLayer(shadowLayer, firstSymbolId);   // before any symbol layer

// 3️⃣ add the actual icon layer
map.addLayer(iconLayer, firstSymbolId);    // same insertion point
```

`firstSymbolId` is the id of the first existing symbol layer (labels).  
If you omit the second argument, the layer goes on top of everything and will hide the basemap’s place‑name labels.

---

## 6. Fine‑tune colour & halo for *any* imagery

| Situation | Recommended `icon-color` | Recommended `icon-halo-color` |
|-----------|--------------------------|------------------------------|
| **General** (mixed land‑cover) | **Amber** `#e68a00` or **Terracotta** `#c94c1e` – high chroma, neutral hue | `rgba(0,0,0,0.75)` (dark, 75 % opacity) |
| **Very bright desert / snow** | Keep the same amber/terracotta (still high chroma) | **Light** halo `rgba(255,255,255,0.8)` if you notice the dark halo blends into the snow. You can switch halo colour by zoom or by a data‑driven expression that checks the underlying raster’s brightness (see `["case", ["<", ["band", "satellite", 0], 0.3], "rgba(255,255,255,0.8)", "rgba(0,0,0,0.75)"]`). |
| **Night‑time imagery** (if you ever use it) | White `#ffffff` (or bright cyan) | Dark halo still works, but you can invert to a light halo for better contrast. |

**Quick test:** Open the map, press `Shift+Click` on a point, and inspect the computed style in the browser dev‑tools. Verify that `icon-color` and `icon-halo-color` are applied; if the halo is missing, you’re probably using a raster PNG without `sdf:true`.

---

## 7. Accessibility check (WCAG)

The **combined** colour of text/icon + halo must meet a 4.5:1 contrast ratio against *any* background. The dark‑halo + amber combo typically yields ~6:1 even over bright soil, and >8:1 over dark forest. Use the WebAIM Contrast Checker with the **effective colour** (icon colour blended over the halo). If you ever need a larger contrast, increase `icon-halo-width` to 3 px or raise the halo opacity to `0.85`.

---

## 8. Full example – ready to paste into a style

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "satellite": {
      "type": "raster",
      "tiles": ["https://mytileserver.com/sat/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg"],
      "tileSize": 256
    },
    "my-points-src": {
      "type": "geojson",
      "data": "data/points.geojson"
    }
  },
  "sprite": "https://mycdn.com/sprites/sprite",   // host your own sprite (see skill)
  "layers": [
    { "id": "background", "type": "background", "paint": { "background-color": "#000000" } },

    // 1️⃣ raster imagery
    { "id": "satellite", "type": "raster", "source": "satellite" },

    // 2️⃣ optional contact‑shadow (below the icon)
    {
      "id": "point-shadow",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "my-points-src",
      "layout": {
        "icon-image": "circle-sdf",
        "icon-size": 0.6,
        "icon-allow-overlap": true,
        "icon-translate": [0, 6],
        "icon-translate-anchor": "map"
      },
      "paint": {
        "icon-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.4)",
        "icon-halo-width": 0
      }
    },

    // 3️⃣ the actual point icons
    {
      "id": "my-points",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "my-points-src",
      "layout": {
        "icon-image": "marker-sdf",
        "icon-size": 1,
        "icon-allow-overlap": true,
        "icon-ignore-placement": false
      },
      "paint": {
        "icon-color": "#e68a00",
        "icon-halo-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.75)",
        "icon-halo-width": 2,
        "icon-halo-blur": 0.5
      }
    },

    // 4️⃣ basemap labels (inserted automatically by the original style)
    // ... keep them after your data layers
  ]
}
```

---

## 9. Checklist – did you cover everything?

- [ ] **Icons are SDF** (`sdf: true`) so `icon-halo-*` works.  
- [ ] **Halo colour** is dark semi‑transparent (`rgba(0,0,0,0.75)`) for most imagery.  
- [ ] **Halo width** is 1.5–2 px (adjust for very small icons).  
- [ ] **Icon fill colour** is a high‑chroma, neutral hue (amber, terracotta, teal).  
- [ ] **Contact‑shadow** (optional) added as a separate layer below the icon.  
- [ ] **Layer order**: imagery → optional shadow → icons → basemap symbols.  
- [ ] **Sprite hosted on your own domain** (no raw‑github URLs).  
- [ ] **Contrast test** passes WCAG AA for the combined icon+halo.  

If you follow the steps above, your green‑/brown‑camouflaged markers will become **bright, edged, and readable on any satellite or aerial background**, while still looking clean and cartographically sound. Happy mapping!

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste ready** example that shows how to:

1. Add a button to the page.  
2. Listen for the click.  
3. Call `map.flyTo()` with a set of options that give a smooth, “camera‑like” transition to the new location.  
4. (Optional) Run code after the animation finishes.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – smooth flyTo on button click</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
  <link
    href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css"
    rel="stylesheet"
  />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; height:100vh; display:flex; flex-direction:column; }
    #map { flex:1; }
    .fly-btn {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px;
      left: 10px;
      padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
      border: 1px solid #777;
      border-radius: 4px;
      cursor: pointer;
      font-family: sans-serif;
    }
    .fly-btn:focus { outline: 2px solid #0066ff; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div id="map"></div>
  <button id="flyBtn" class="fly-btn" aria-label="Fly to Paris">
    Fly to Paris
  </button>

  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <script>
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1️⃣ Initialise the map (replace the source URLs with your own)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: 'map',
      style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any vector style you like
      center: [-74.006, 40.7128], // New York City (lon, lat)
      zoom: 10,
      pitch: 0,
      bearing: 0
    });

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣ Define the target camera parameters
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    const target = {
      // lon/lat of the new centre – Paris in this case
      center: [2.3522, 48.8566],

      // Optional: zoom, bearing, pitch – adjust to your design
      zoom: 13,
      bearing: 30,   // rotate the map a little for visual interest
      pitch: 45,     // tilt the view so you see a 3‑D perspective

      // -----------------------------------------------------------------
      // 3️⃣ Fly‑to animation options
      // -----------------------------------------------------------------
      // duration (ms) – total time of the animation
      duration: 2500,

      // curve – how “curvy” the flight path is (default 1.42)
      // lower = tighter, higher = more gentle
      curve: 1.2,

      // easing – a function that receives a t∈[0,1] and returns eased t.
      // The default is a cubic‑ease‑out; you can supply your own.
      // e.g. t => t < 0.5 ? 2*t*t : -1+(4-2*t)*t
      // easing: t => t,

      // essential – set true for browsers that respect reduced‑motion
      // preferences (see Accessibility section below)
      essential: true
    };

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣ Click handler – fire the animation
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    const btn = document.getElementById('flyBtn');
    btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
      // If you want to block user interaction while the camera moves:
      map.dragPan.disable();
      map.scrollZoom.disable();
      map.boxZoom.disable();
      map.keyboard.disable();

      map.flyTo(target);

      // When the animation ends, re‑enable interaction and run any
      // post‑flight logic you need.
      map.once('moveend', () => {
        map.dragPan.enable();
        map.scrollZoom.enable();
        map.boxZoom.enable();
        map.keyboard.enable();

        console.log('Arrived at Paris!');
        // e.g. open a popup, load a new data source, etc.
      });
    });

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣ Accessibility – respect “prefers-reduced‑motion”
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Browsers that honour the user’s reduced‑motion setting will
    // automatically skip the animation if `essential` is false.
    // If you want to *always* animate, set essential: true (as above).
    // If you want to *never* animate for those users, you can detect it:
    const prefersReduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');
    if (prefersReduced.matches) {
      // Override the default: make the flight instantaneous
      btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        map.jumpTo({ center: target.center, zoom: target.zoom, bearing: target.bearing, pitch: target.pitch });
      });
    }
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## Why `flyTo` and not `easeTo`?

| Method | Typical use case |
|--------|------------------|
| **`flyTo`** | You want a *camera‑like* motion that follows a curved path, changes altitude (zoom) and bearing together, and feels like “flying” over the earth. |
| **`easeTo`** | You only need a straight‑line interpolation (no altitude curve) – e.g. panning a little or zooming in place. |
| **`jumpTo`** | Instant change, no animation (useful for users who request reduced motion). |

`flyTo` automatically computes a smooth “flight arc” based on the start and end zoom levels, so you rarely need to calculate the intermediate points yourself.

---

## Key Options (quick reference)

| Option | What it does | Typical value |
|--------|--------------|---------------|
| `center` | `[lon, lat]` of the new focal point | `[2.3522, 48.8566]` |
| `zoom` | Target zoom level (higher = closer) | `13` |
| `bearing` | Rotation clockwise from north (degrees) | `30` |
| `pitch` | Tilt of the camera (degrees) | `45` |
| `duration` | Total animation time (ms) | `2500` |
| `curve` | Shape of the flight path (default 1.42) | `1.2` (tighter) |
| `easing` | Custom easing function (`t => …`) | *optional* |
| `essential` | If `false`, respects `prefers-reduced-motion` | `true` (force animation) |

---

## Accessibility notes (important for production)

1. **Respect reduced‑motion** – set `essential: false` (the default) or detect the media query yourself and fall back to `jumpTo`.  
2. **Keyboard focus** – the button is a native `<button>` element, so it receives focus and can be activated with `Enter` or `Space`.  
3. **ARIA label** – the `aria-label` attribute gives a screen‑reader a clear description of what will happen.  
4. **Contrast** – the button’s background (`rgba(255,255,255,0.9)`) meets WCAG AA contrast against most basemaps; adjust if you use a dark basemap.  

---

### TL;DR

```js
map.flyTo({
  center: [lon, lat],
  zoom: 13,
  bearing: 30,
  pitch: 45,
  duration: 2500,
  curve: 1.2,
  essential: true   // set false to honor reduced‑motion
});
```

Hook that call to any UI event (button click, menu selection, etc.) and you’ll get a smooth, cinematic transition that works on both light‑vector and satellite/imagery basemaps. Happy mapping!
